{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"lousy-unsuited-gnu","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Summary\n\nResolving a single ticket id falls through to `_scan_alias` (`src/rebar/_ids.py:312`), which\n`listdir`s the whole store and `json.load`s every ticket's CREATE/SNAPSHOT event — roughly **8,900\nJSON parses to resolve ONE id** on this store. The consequence is that reading a SINGLE ticket is\nabout **2.5x more expensive than compiling the entire store**.\n\n## Measured (2026-09-05, live server, post-projection-fix build)\n\n| operation | latency |\n|---|---|\n| `ready_tickets` — whole-store read | 16.3 s |\n| `show_ticket` — single ticket, server IDLE | **41.7 s** |\n| `show_ticket` — single ticket, 8 heavy reads in flight | **80.5 s** |\n\nA single-ticket read being 2.5x a whole-store compile is the inversion that makes this a defect\nrather than a slow path.\n\n## Why this matters beyond latency\n\nThe MCP client deadline is ~60 s. An idle `show_ticket` at 41.7 s is already close to it, and under\nconcurrent load it goes **over**. That is not a cosmetic timeout:\n\n- A gate call that times out is the AMBIGUOUS signal that provokes a duplicate, double-billed gate\n  run (bug `d80d`). The de-dup protection exists precisely because this signal is ambiguous.\n- It is also a silently-dropped WRITE. Observed repeatedly tonight: `comment_ticket` calls exceeded\n  120 s and were backgrounded, and one FAILED outright after 300 s with \"sent no response or\n  progress\". The caller's write appears issued and simply never lands. Three separate agents hit\n  this independently in one session and all fell back to the local CLI, which completed the same\n  writes immediately.\n- It blocked filing THIS bug: the discovering agent's `create_ticket` aborted at the 300 s MCP idle\n  timeout, so the finding had to be carried out by hand.\n\n## Relationship to `witted-invisible-roan` (9ea3)\n\n9ea3 tracks \"MCP endpoint returns persistent HTTP 502 during long gate jobs\". That symptom now\nappears to have TWO independent causes, and they should not be conflated:\n\n1. **Memory** — `reduce_all_tickets` materialises ~243 MB of full ticket state per call, offloaded\n   through a 40-slot `anyio` thread limiter, giving `RSS = 69 MB + K x 295 MB` and an OOM before\n   plateau on a 7.6 GiB host. Fix in flight (per-ticket projection, 295 -> 84 MB).\n2. **Latency — THIS bug.** Independent of memory: a single idle read already burns 41.7 s of a 60 s\n   budget before any concurrency.\n\nFixing (1) does not fix (2). A `show_ticket` that costs a full-store scan will keep crossing the\ndeadline whenever the host is contended.\n\n## Suggested direction (not prescriptive)\n\nResolve ids from an INDEX rather than by scanning: the store already compiles alias/id mappings\nelsewhere, and a persisted or cached alias->id map would turn ~8,900 parses into one lookup. Before\nbuilding one, confirm which callers reach `_scan_alias` and why the fast paths ahead of it miss —\nthe fall-through may itself be the bug, i.e. an index may already exist and simply not be consulted\nfor the forms agents actually pass (aliases and short ids).\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1 The fall-through to `_scan_alias` is explained: which id FORMS reach it, and whether a\n      faster path already exists that is not being consulted. Answer before designing an index.\n- [ ] AC2 A RED benchmark/test demonstrates the current cost (single-ticket resolve doing O(store)\n      parses) and fails for that reason before any fix.\n- [ ] AC3 Single-ticket read latency is brought well under the ~60 s client deadline with headroom\n      under concurrent load, measured on a store of this size (~8,900 tickets), not a fixture.\n- [ ] AC4 Resolution remains CORRECT across every accepted id form — full canonical id, 8-digit\n      two-quad short id, alias, and the deprecated 4-digit fragment — proven by test, not assumed.\n- [ ] AC5 The measured before/after latency table is recorded on this ticket.\n\n## Constraints\n\nNever use `--force` or any gate bypass. `timeout` does not exist on this macOS host (exit 127) —\nbound with `perl -e 'alarm N; exec @ARGV'`. Do not touch the shared main checkout; do not\n`git stash` anywhere. Terraform may be written and validated but NEVER applied.\n\n## Attribution\n\nMechanism and all latency measurements were produced by the agent investigating `9ea3`; this ticket\nwas filed by the orchestrator because the MCP `create_ticket` path was itself unavailable.","id":"94c5-b42e-ac80-4f06","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Resolving one ticket id scans the whole store: ~8,900 JSON parses, 41.7s idle single-ticket read"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788641155133567001,"uuid":"c64e7d93-8671-4a85-aa99-2f5e8e63384c"}